  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Express a continuous-time signal as a continuum of shifted impulses using the sifting identity $x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A shifted impulse such as $\\delta(t-\\tau)$ is located at the time selected by $\\tau$. The sifting property says that, inside an integral, this impulse keeps only the value of the weighting function at the matching time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau=f(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this formula, $\\tau$ is the integration variable and $t$ is the time where the result is being evaluated. The impulse $\\delta(t-\\tau)$ selects $\\tau=t$, so the integral returns the weighting function evaluated at $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a signal $x(t)$, use the same rule with the weighting function $x(\\tau)$. This gives the continuous-time sifting representation:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the integral as a continuous superposition of shifted impulses. Each value of $\\tau$ supplies one shifted impulse $\\delta(t-\\tau)$, and the signal value $x(\\tau)$ supplies that impulse's weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is not ordinary area accumulation over wide pulses. It is a way to rebuild the signal from impulse components indexed by $\\tau$. That decomposition prepares the input signal for later system-output construction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating a Sifting Integral at a Fixed Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}(\\tau^2+1)\\delta(3-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(3-\\tau)$. This is the sifting pattern with the fixed time value $3$, so the impulse selects the value $\\tau=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The weighting expression is the factor multiplying the impulse:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(\\tau)=\\tau^2+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "By the sifting property, replace $\\tau$ in the weighting expression with the selected value $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}(\\tau^2+1)\\delta(3-\\tau)\\,d\\tau &= f(3) \\\\\n&=3^2+1 \\\\\n&=10\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the value of the sifting integral is $10$. The integration variable $\\tau$ has been selected and removed, so the result is a scalar.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing $x(t)$ with $\\delta$(t-$\\tau$)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the impulse $\\delta(t-\\tau)$. Instead of selecting a fixed number such as $\\tau=3$, this impulse selects the symbolic value $\\tau=t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The expression multiplying the impulse is the signal expression in $\\tau$:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(\\tau)=e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the sifting property by replacing every occurrence of $\\tau$ in that expression with $t$. The integration variable $\\tau$ is removed, while $t$ remains as the output time variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau &= f(t) \\\\\n&= e^{-2t}u(t)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the sifting integral reconstructs the signal expression $e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing a Signal as a Sifting Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write $x(t)=\\cos(2t)u(t)$ as a continuous-time sifting integral.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phrase \"as a continuous-time sifting integral.\" This means use the reconstruction identity, but build the integral instead of simplifying one.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the given signal expression and rewrite it as a function of the integration variable $\\tau$. Replace every $t$ in the signal expression with $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=\\cos(2\\tau)u(\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now place $x(\\tau)$ as the weight multiplying $\\delta(t-\\tau)$. Use infinite limits because no support restriction has been requested.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\cos(2\\tau)u(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This integral is the sifting representation of the original signal $x(t)=\\cos(2t)u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time Sifting Representation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Restricting the Sifting Integral to the Signal Support",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the signal $x(t)=2t$ for $0\\le t\\le 3$, and $x(t)=0$ otherwise, as a sifting integral with limits restricted to its support.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the limits should be restricted to the signal support. The signal is nonzero only on $0\\le t\\le 3$, so the integration variable should range over the same interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le \\tau \\le 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On that interval, the signal formula is $x(t)=2t$. Rewrite the nonzero formula with $\\tau$ replacing $t$ to form the weight in the sifting integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=2\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Place this weight in front of $\\delta(t-\\tau)$, and use the support interval as the lower and upper limits. Values of $\\tau$ outside this interval contribute zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\int_{0}^{3}2\\tau\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the support-restricted sifting representation of the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
